    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Hussites followed the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-hus">Jan Hus</a>, burned at the stake at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> on 6 July 1415, and took the chalice of wine given to the laity as their emblem.</li><li>They were not one movement but several parties: moderate Utraquists, who wished to leave the order of the Church untouched, and radical Taborites, Orebites and Orphans, who held to Wycliffe’s teaching and wished to remove the hierarchy.</li><li>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> and civil wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussite-wars">Hussite Wars</a> (1420–1434) the Utraquists came out dominant, Catholics and Utraquists were given legal equality in 1485, and Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> was reimposed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> only after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a> in 1620.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The name Tábor was a play on words: in Czech it meant a camp or encampment, and it was also the traditional name of the mountain on which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> was expected to return.</p></aside>
    </section>

